<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veera Ballala II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1173">1173</a>–<a href="/years/1220">1220</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Vira Ballala II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veera Ballala II was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1173 to 1220. He declared full independence from the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> around 1187, defeated the Seuna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a> and made the Hoysalas the leading power of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, later intervening in the Tamil country. His reign saw the building of temples in the Hoysala style and the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veera-ballala-ii.jpg" alt="Veera Ballala II" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dineshkannambadi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Profile_of_Amrutesvara_temple_at_Amruthapura_in_Chikkamagaluru_district.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ballala came to the throne on 22 July 1173 after a successful rebellion against his father, Narasimha I, with the help of malnad chiefs such as the Chengalvas and Kongalvas, whom he later made sure could not rise against him. His son, Prince Vira Narasimha II, and his crowned queen Umadevi supported him in war and government. Another queen, Cholamahadevi, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> princess, and his daughter Somaladevi married the Chola king Kulothunga Chola III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As the Western Chalukya throne weakened, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Kalachuris contended for its lands. Ballala annexed Hangal around 1178, but around 1179 the Kalachuri commander Sankama defeated him and seized it. The decisive rivalry for the Krishna-Tungabhadra doab was with the Yadava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhillama-v">Bhillama V</a>, who took Basavakalyana by about 1189. Ballala defeated the exiled Chalukya king Someshvara VI at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a> around 1190, beat Bhillama at Soratur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakkundi">Lakkundi</a> around 1191, and pushed his frontier to the Malaprabha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers; by about 1192 he had crowned himself emperor over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> region.</p><p>After about 1212 he lost all his lands north of the Tungabhadra to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhana-ii">Singhana II</a>. Around 1216 Maravarman Sundara Pandya drove Kulothunga III into exile, and Ballala sent Narasimha II, who repulsed the Pandyas and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> kingdom. Ballala then took titles such as Cholarajyapratishtacharya, establisher of the Chola kingdom, and Dakshina Chakravarti, emperor of the south, and brought part of the Kaveri plain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a> under direct rule, leaving an army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His court included the Jain poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janna">Janna</a>, given the title Kavichakravarthi, whose Yashodhara Charitre of about 1209 deals with Jain tenets; Nemichandra, author of the Lilavati Prabandha; and the Brahman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudrabhatta">Rudrabhatta</a>, whose Jagannatha Vijaya of about 1180 is the earliest surviving Vaishnava epic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>. Temples of his reign in the Vesara style include the Kedareshwara temple at Halebidu, the Veera Narayana temple at Belavadi, the Amrutesvara Temple at Amruthapura and the twin Chennakeshava and Nageshvara temples at Mosale. In 1174 he gave Meruhalli village and other lands to feed Jain monks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veera Ballala II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marital alliances bound the Cholas to the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi, which remained largely independent under Chola protection, and Kulothunga Chola III allied with the Hoysala <b>Veera Ballala II</b> through a marriage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Full independence came only under his grandson <b>Veera Ballala II</b>, who threw off Chalukya and Kalachuri overlordship and assumed imperial titles in 1187.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seuna Yadava ruler Bhillama V took the northern provinces and Kalyani, while the Hoysala <b>Veera Ballala II</b> defeated the Chalukya forces in the south, and Someshvara IV disappears from the records c. 1189.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
